at this point, there's virtually nothing left that hasnt been wikipedia'd
hehe, yeah...
anyway, what were you referring to?
you mean the hen-egg-situation for the protocol support?
yup
i'm not convinced at this point that cachelogic will fair any better than joltid did
or that we shouldnt just approach isps ourselves
there are soo many out there, esp. in non-regulated markets
there are legal issues as well
particularly in the us
don't they have exceptions for ISPs? iirc they aren't liable for anything until they're notified of illegal content they're hosting?
yes, there are Safe Harbor protections, but it's unclear (and so far no one's willing to risk) if that still holds for willfully caching copyrighted material
all tha law in this area is still quite gray :)
and then there is the required investment for the caching hardware...
hardware costs are quite minimal
peanuts
particularly compared to bandwidth costs
hrmhrm, ok... i don't know how much they've to pay for transit traffic...
depends on the isp of course
but IXP and peering traffic is supposed to be quite cheap
at least that's what a guy from a local ISP told me
frankly, i highly doubt we'll ever see generic bt caches inside isps
more likely we'll see isps do deals like NTL did
use bt as a VOD delivery method
for &quot;legit&quot; content only
then caches won't have the desired effect, since much of the traffic is caused by illegal content...
nobody wants to get their hands anywhere near illegal content
bsuiness wise
see limewire got sued?
well, iirc the JPC caching method was content-agnostic and from what you've told me the cachelogic one isn't...
yeah, i know
but it might be easier to test things like that in a less stringent jurisdiction where ISPs still have problems with BT traffic
maybe canada? ^^
it's not so much that content can or cannot be obvisously identified
it's that they &quot;know&quot; most of it will be illegal
and dont want to be sued
i see :/
they are legally OK now, even if it does cost them hundreds of K (or millions) of $ in bandwidth
small potatoes compared to the court cases and settlements these days
it's all a big crazy mess
summa sumarum: there are quite some obstacles for caching solutions
indeed ^^
technical is the least of the issues :)
best solution: ipv6, mcast for every node and p2p applications spreading stuff via mcast... that would reduce transit traffic tremendously at the price of routing overhead
