looks
closing-frame is part of binary-frame in that version
ah, right
again, hard to read
I wonder how the protocol can be really used in isp webservers
the recent changes got rid of all the binary blobs and stuff that people complained about, so hopefully it's a little easier now
I doubt I can reserve any ports for my use
you mean web hosting providers?
yeah
dreamhost allow me to run arbitrary scripts that open any port above 1024
dunno about others
interesting
surprising
if you can run arbitrary code on unix, it's pretty hard to stop it, as i understand it
short of a firewall
dunno how common that is
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: still another thing, which was mentioned in hybi list
the need for bigint
do we really need to be able to handle huge binary frames?
you can just bail if you get a length that's too big
i don't see why people would send long lengths today
but when everyone is using 256 bit chips and petabit ethernet to the house, i don't see why we'd want to rev the entire protocol
well, that is something to mention in the spec, that bailing out is ok
it says &quot;WARNING: User agents may impose implementation-specific
limits on the lengths of invalid frames that they will skip. &quot;
